  - The Javadoc author tag must contain only the student's u-Kürzel.
  - Follow rules for variable, method, and package naming.
  - The second entry must be one of the five document types: image, audio, video, text, or program.
  - Ties in sorting are broken lexicographically: `defined` before `undefined`; multivalued tags by
  tag-value lexicographic order; documents by document identifier lexicographically.
  - Avoid excessively long methods and files (no explicit numeric limit given).
  - Program source code should be written in English.
  - Plan time for the first submission because mandatory tests gate further scoring.
  - Refer to the ILIAS Wiki for modeling guidance and grading criteria.
  - Tag identifiers must be unique in type: a tag is either binary, multivalued, or numeric; mixed
  tags are forbidden.
  - Each information-gain line begins with a slash, then slash-separated prior tag=value pairs, then
   another slash, then the tag=information-gain in Java decimal notation rounded to two decimals.
  - Insert a line with three hyphens `---` as a separator between the information-gain part and the
  document list part.
  - The output order of documents is descending by p(D_{t=v}, D).
  - Automatically generated tags must also not be defined multiple times on a document (e.g., images
   must not have both `size` and `imagesize`).
  - The `run <id>` command executes the automatic structuring on the loaded input with the given id;
   any access count changes since loading are taken into account.
  - Multivalued and numerical tags may be set at most once per document; multiple definitions
  produce an error.
  - Binary tags (labels) are given only by their identifier and have no explicit value.
  - Use System.out for standard output unless otherwise specified.
  - Values of numerical tags must be integers within the integer range.
  - Values of multivalued tags must start with a letter and otherwise consist of letters, digits,
  and possibly spaces.
  - Tag identifiers must always be output in lowercase in the specified output format.
  - First output the computed information gains line by line for those greater than ε = 10^-3,
  sorted within a decision step in descending order by information gain.
  - After printing an error message the program must continue and wait for the next input.
  - Do not reassign the standard input or output streams.
  - During command execution, if executing a command would violate the specification the program
  must print a meaningful error message.
  - Tag identifiers are case-insensitive (e.g., length, Length, lenGth denote the same tag).
  - Valid tag identifiers begin with a Latin letter and otherwise consist of Latin letters and
  digits.
  - Any other values that do not match the allowed tag formats cause an error.
  - The `change <id> <file> <number>` command modifies only the state of a loaded input (not the
  source text file) and on success prints `Change <number> to <number> for <file>` showing old then
  new access count.
  - Some Checkstyle rules are specially marked and violating them causes the submission to be scored
